A||||6||Adrift||Difference in quality for rebuild calipers?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-11-2001||01:17 AM||||I pulled off my rear calipers to get a pair of rebuilts ones and called around for prices, here's waht I got:<P>GM - $405 each<BR>Canadian Tire - $90ish each<BR>Racetech - $120 each<BR>NAPA - $70 each<P>Is there a large difference in the quality of the parts between different vendors or is a caliper, well, a caliper?||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||12-11-2001||08:17 AM||||Wow! Big difference!  You don't state model but as to quality difference, there shouldn't be any.  NAPA is probably as good as it gets.<P>Now, to pricing. I have an '88.  Best rebuild pricing I could find was about $120 USD.<P>'88 rears are the most expensive to replace.  Hopefully, you have an earlier model.  Otherwise, I have overspent for mine!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001107&p=57211671&Sequence=0"> <BR>You can have all the SPEED you can afford!||||reg||9
Z||000002||GTDude||12-11-2001||08:27 AM||||There are only 4 actual parts to a normal 1 piston caliper:  The housing...the piston...the interior seal ....and the dust seal.  On a normal caliper any idiot can rebuild one correctly.  However, on the rear caliper of a fiero, the park brake is made into the caliper.  This is where things can get tricky cause the piston is a lot more complicated.  So, there can definitely be differences in qualities of rebuilds.  Fortunately for you....im an auto tech...i used to own a NAPA store...and I've had rebuilt NAPA calipers on my Fiero for the past 3 years......no problems.  I also know the lengths that NAPA/RAYLOC go to to make sure that their rebuilt brake parts meet the highest standards of quality.  Obviously, from my experience...and the price diff...I would buy NAPA calipers.  Hope this helps.<P>GTDude<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||NEPTUNE||12-11-2001||08:47 AM||||I just bought a rebuilt rear caliper for my 87 at Autozone. $33.00 plus $80.00 core charge.(USD) Seems to work OK after 2 weeks. 88 parts probably cost more. Front and rear are different price.<p>[This message has been edited by NEPTUNE (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Adrift||12-11-2001||11:11 AM||||Turns out Napa is $101, The other price I listed was for front calipers they got mixed up - should I still go with them even though Canadian Tire is significantly cheaper? (I called them again - they are actually $80 from them, which means I'll save $40)<P>(By the way these are for a 86)<p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Adrift||12-11-2001||12:02 PM||||BUMP<BR>(Want to buy them later today)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Banzai||12-11-2001||04:55 PM||||I purchased calipers from a local store called Murry's Discount Auto Parts.  I've been dealing with them for about 9 years. I paid 12.99 for the fronts and 59.99 for the rears. Well I've had to replace one front(sticking) and one rear(pitted sealing surface at hose fitting). I think that the left front is sticking too but I have to get an alignment to be sure. The fronts are really easy to rebuild. I painted mine so any sandblasting work that was already done was a big help.( I went through 1500lbs. on the rest of the car) For 12.99 I bought them. That price also included new bushings, bleeder valve and cap and a new banjo bolt and washers. But still you get what you pay for I'd go with the NAPA rears and find someone else for the fronts. Some calipers may come "loaded". That means they have pads and all new hardware included.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
